Nest Result.Publisher directly in Result instead of Result.OCombine.Publisher

Apple's Combine declares this publisher as `Result.Publisher` -- the guest SDK's
Combine.swiftinterface, x86_64-apple-macos slice, declares the type at lines 1727-1739
(`extension Swift.Result { ... public struct Publisher : Combine.Publisher { ... } }`)
and `Just.setFailureType(to:)` at line 592 returns
`Swift.Result<Output, E>.Publisher`. OpenCombine instead nests the same type under its
`OCombine` disambiguation namespace, so the mangled name of the type and of every
function returning it carries `OCombine` and no client compiled against Apple's
interface can bind them. Measured on the ladder: XCSourceControl.framework demands
    _$ss6ResultO7CombineE9PublisherVMa / VMn / Vyxq__GAcdCMc
    _$s7Combine4JustV14setFailureType2tos6ResultOAAE9PublisherVyxqd___Gqd__m_ts5ErrorRd__lF
and the module exports the same four names only with `8OCombineV` spliced in.

Darling builds OpenCombine as the module `Combine` (build-arch.sh), so there is no
second module to disambiguate from and the extra nesting is pure noise. This patch
moves the struct (and its private `Inner` subscription) out of `OCombine` to `Result`
and repoints every `Result<...>.OCombine.Publisher` in the module at it, so one type
carries both the Apple name and the existing OpenCombine call sites.

`OCombine` itself and the `ocombine` accessor stay (they are OpenCombine's own public
API), and Optional keeps its own `Optional.OCombine.Publisher` untouched: Apple's
`Optional.Publisher` is a separate name that no image in this closure demands. The
`#if !canImport(Combine)` block that declared
`typealias Publisher = OCombine.Publisher` is deleted -- it is excluded in every build
of this module, and after this patch its body no longer names anything.

